# DTO Rules

DTOs are simple data containers for moving data across layer boundaries.
They live at the Interface layer edge — input from HTTP, output to HTTP.

## Request DTOs
```go
// controller/dto/create_order_request.go
package dto

import "github.com/go-playground/validator/v10"

type CreateOrderReq struct {
    ProductID string  `json:"product_id" binding:"required,uuid"`
    Quantity  int     `json:"quantity"   binding:"required,min=1,max=100"`
    Note      string  `json:"note"       binding:"omitempty,max=500"`
}
```

## Response DTOs
```go
// controller/dto/order_response.go
package dto

import "time"

// Never include: password, refresh_token, internal IDs, PII beyond what's needed
type OrderResp struct {
    ID          string    `json:"id"`
    Status      string    `json:"status"`
    TotalAmount int64     `json:"total_amount"`
    CreatedAt   time.Time `json:"created_at"`
}

// Paginated response wrapper
type PaginatedResp[T any] struct {
    Data  []T   `json:"data"`
    Total int64 `json:"total"`
    Page  int   `json:"page"`
    Limit int   `json:"limit"`
}
```

## Rules
- Request DTOs use `binding` tags for validation (GIN)
- Response DTOs use `json` tags only — no binding, no db tags
- Never put `gorm:` or `db:` tags on DTOs
- Never embed domain entities in DTOs
- Use `omitempty` for optional response fields
- PaginatedResp is generic — reuse for all list endpoints
